(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_solutions=[{id:"neet-2026r-q059",pn:59,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Solutions",st:"Deviation from Raoult's law",q:`Consider the following statements about the solutions formed by mixing two liquids.
A. An ideal solution thus formed obeys Raoult\u2019s law throughout the composition range.
B. Mixture of chloroform and acetone shows negative deviation from Raoult\u2019s law.
C. Mixture of aniline and phenol shows positive deviation from Raoult\u2019s law.`,o:["B and C only","A only","A and C only","A and B only"],c:3,s:`In phenol and aniline, the hydrogen bond between the phenolic proton and the lone pair on the nitrogen of aniline is stronger than the hydrogen bonds between similar molecules.
So the mixture shows negative deviation, not positive. Statement C is wrong.
Statements A and B are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Concentration terms and Henry's law",q:`Identify the correct statements :
(A) The molality of 2.5 g of ethanoic acid (Molar mass : 60 g mol\u207B\xB9) in 75 g of benzene solution is 0.556 m.
(B) The molarity of a solution containing 5 g of NaOH (molar mass : 40 g mol\u207B\xB9) in 450 mL of solution is 0.278 M at 298 K.
(C) Aquatic species are more comfortable in cold water.
(D) The solubility of gas increases with decrease in pressure.
(E) For a binary mixture of A and B, the number of moles of A and B are nA and nB respectively. The mole fraction of B will be x_B = nA/(nA \xD7 nB).
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A and C only","A, B and C only","A, D and E only","A and B only"],c:1,s:`(A) Molality = (2.5 \xD7 1000)/(60 \xD7 75) = 0.556 molal. Correct.
(B) Molarity = (5 \xD7 1000)/(40 \xD7 450) = 0.278 M. Correct.
(C) Henry's constant K_H increases with temperature, so gas solubility falls as temperature rises. Cold water holds more dissolved oxygen, so aquatic species are more comfortable in it. Correct.
(D) By Henry's law p = K_H x, so solubility increases when pressure increases. Statement D is incorrect.
(E) x_B = nB/(nA + nB), not nA/(nA + nB). Statement E is incorrect.
So A, B and C are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Non-ideal solutions, Raoult's law",q:"Mixture of chloroform and acetone forms a solution with negative deviation from Raoult's law due to :",o:["Formation of hydrogen bonding between acetone and chloroform","Increase in escaping tendency of molecules of each component.","Stronger intermolecular forces between chloroform molecules than those between chloroform and acetone molecules.","Repulsive forces."],c:0,s:`Acetone and chloroform show negative deviation from Raoult's law because of stronger H-bonding between the two in the mixture.
The H-bond is formed between the C=O oxygen of acetone and the H of chloroform (C=O ----- H\u2013CCl\u2083).
So the escaping tendency decreases.
Therefore the vapour pressure decreases and the boiling point increases.`,sfig:{w:300,h:96,alt:"Acetone (CH3 and H3C on a C double bonded to O) with a dashed line from its O to the H of chloroform, H\u2013C bonded to three Cl.",d:[["t",10,14,"Acetone",""],["t",200,14,"Chloroform",""],["t",26,42,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",26,84,"H\u2083C","m"],["t",66,62,"C","m"],["l",40,40,58,52,""],["l",40,76,58,64,""],["l",74,60,88,60,""],["l",74,56,88,56,""],["t",97,62,"O","m"],["l",106,58,160,58,"d"],["t",168,62,"H","m"],["l",176,58,190,58,""],["t",198,62,"C","m"],["l",206,52,220,40,""],["l",206,58,222,58,""],["l",206,64,220,76,""],["t",232,40,"Cl","m"],["t",232,62,"Cl","m"],["t",232,84,"Cl","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q080",pn:80,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Solutions",st:"Ideal solutions",q:`Assertion A: For an ideal solution formed by mixing liquids P and Q, \u0394mixH = 0 and \u0394mixV = 0
Reason R: No interactions occur between P and Q
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:1,s:`For an ideal solution, \u0394mixH = 0 and \u0394mixV = 0, so A is correct.
The energy needed to break P\u2013P and Q\u2013Q interactions equals the energy released on forming P\u2013Q interactions.
So interactions do occur between P and Q, and R is not correct.`},{id:"neet-2025-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Solutions",st:"Types of solutions",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I (Example) \u2192 List-II (Type of Solution)
A. Humidity \u2014 I. Solid in solid
B. Alloys \u2014 II. Liquid in gas
C. Amalgams \u2014 III. Solid in gas
D. Smoke \u2014 IV. Liquid in solid
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:1,s:`Humidity is a solution of liquid in gas.
Alloy is a solution of solid in solid.
Amalgam is a solution of liquid in solid.
Smoke is a solution of solid in gas.`},{id:"neet-2025-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Solutions",st:"Elevation of boiling point",q:"Which of the following aqueous solution will exhibit highest boiling point?",o:["0.01M Urea","0.01M KNO\u2083","0.01M Na\u2082SO\u2084","0.015M C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086"],c:2,s:`\u0394T_b = i K_b \xD7 m
\u0394T_b \u221D i \xD7 m
Take molarity as same as molality.
(1) 0.01M Urea: i \xD7 m = 1 \xD7 0.01 = 0.01
(2) 0.01M KNO\u2083: i \xD7 m = 2 \xD7 0.01 = 0.02
(3) 0.01M Na\u2082SO\u2084: i \xD7 m = 3 \xD7 0.01 = 0.03
(4) 0.015M C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086: i \xD7 m = 1 \xD7 0.015 = 0.015
T_b' = T_b* + \u0394T_b
Higher the value of (i \xD7 m), higher is the boiling point.`},{id:"neet-2025-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Solutions",st:"Raoult's law deviations",q:"5 moles of liquid X and 10 moles of liquid Y make a solution having a vapour pressure of 70 torr. The vapour pressures of pure X and Y are 63 torr and 78 torr respectively. Which of the following is true regarding the described solution?",o:["The solution shows positive deviation.","The solution shows negative deviation.","The solution is ideal.","The solution has volume greater than the sum of individual volumes."],c:1,s:`P_total = X_x P\xB0_x + X_y P\xB0_y
= (5/15) \xD7 63 + (10/15) \xD7 78
= 21 + 52
= 73 torr
Observed total pressure of the solution is 70 torr.
It is less than the calculated total pressure.
Hence, it shows negative deviation.`},{id:"neet-2024-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Henry's law",q:"The Henry's law constant (K_H) values of three gases (A, B, C) in water are 145, 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 and 35 kbar, respectively. The solubility of these gases in water follow the order :",o:["A > C > B","A > B > C","B > A > C","B > C > A"],c:3,s:`Henry's law constant is proportional to 1/(solubility of gas).
A higher K_H at a given pressure means a lower solubility.
K_H order: A > C > B.
So solubility order in water: B > C > A.`},{id:"neet-2024-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Osmotic pressure and temperature",q:`The plot of osmotic pressure (\u03A0) vs concentration (mol L\u207B\xB9) for a solution gives a straight line with slope 25.73 L bar mol\u207B\xB9. The temperature at which the osmotic pressure measurement is done is :
(Use R = 0.083 L bar mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)`,o:["25.73\xBAC","12.05\xBAC","37\xBAC","310\xBAC"],c:2,s:`\u03A0 = CRT, so the slope = RT.
25.73 = 0.083 \xD7 T.
T = 25.73/0.083 = 309.47 \u2248 310 K.
Temperature in \xBAC = 310 \u2013 273 = 37\xBAC.`},{id:"neet-2022-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Solutions",st:"Molality",q:"In one molal solution that contains 0.5 mole of a solute, there is",o:["500 g of solvent","100 mL of solvent","1000 g of solvent","500 mL of solvent"],c:0,s:`Molality = moles of solute / mass of solvent in kg.
1 = 0.5 / (mass of solvent in kg).
Mass of solvent = 0.5 kg = 500 g.`}]})();
